Studies have shown that regular physical activity reduces cortisol levels, a key stress hormone, thereby directly lowering stress.
Exercise stimulates the release of endorphins, which are natural mood elevators that counteract the effects of stress.
Improved sleep quality from regular exercise helps the body recover from stress, making it easier to manage daily pressures.
The parasympathetic nervous system, responsible for rest and digestion, is activated during exercise, promoting relaxation and reducing anxiety.
Long-term exercise habits are linked to lower rates of anxiety and depression, which are closely related to chronic stress.